          '**Clinical Indicators of Elder Abuse in This Case**:\n\n**Physical Abuse Indicators**:\n- **Bruises in various stages of healing**: Indicates repeated trauma over weeks.\n- **Pattern injuries**: Grab-mark bruises on upper arms and wrists (finger-shaped). Linear bruise on back (struck with an object). These are NOT consistent with falls.\n- **Location**: Bruises on torso, flank, and back are highly suspicious — falls in elderly typically produce bruises on extremities, bony prominences, and face. Trunk bruises suggest inflicted injury.\n- **Concealment**: Long sleeves in warm weather to hide bruises.\n- **Patient\'s fear response**: Flinching when son touches her. Looking at son before answering. Hesitation when asked about safety.\n\n**Neglect Indicators**:\n- **Weight loss**: 15 lbs in 4 months with low albumin — malnutrition.\n- **Medication withholding**: Son discontinued 3 medications without medical guidance, including antihypertensive (BP now uncontrolled at 168/94), statin, and antidepressant.\n- **Poor hygiene**: Previously well-groomed, now unwashed with dirty fingernails.\n- **Dehydration**: Dry mucous membranes.\n- **Diabetes mismanagement**: A1c rose from 6.8% to 9.2% — suggesting dietary neglect and possible medication mismanagement.\n\n**Financial Exploitation Indicators**:\n- Patient has Social Security and a pension but reports \'no money for groceries.\'\n- Son obtained financial power of attorney after moving in.\n- Son is unemployed and living in her home.\n\n**Psychological/Emotional Abuse Indicators**:\n- Social isolation: Cut off from daughter, church, senior center.\n- Loss of autonomy: Son speaks for her, makes all decisions.\n- Patient\'s fear and deference: \'He knows better than I do.\'\n- Rapid functional decline inconsistent with medical conditions.\n\n**Types of Elder Abuse Present**:\n1. **Physical abuse**: Inflicted injuries (grab marks, linear bruise, restraint marks)\n2. **Neglect**: Malnutrition, dehydration, medication withholding, hygiene neglect\n3. **Financial exploitation**: Misappropriation of income, obtaining POA under questionable circumstances\n4. **Psychological abuse**: Isolation, intimidation, controlling behavior\n\n**Screening Tools**:\n- **Elder Abuse Suspicion Index (EASI)**: 6-question screening tool for primary care. Asks directly about fear, neglect, and exploitation.\n- **Hwalek-Sengstock Elder Abuse Screening Test (H-S/EAST)**: 15-item tool assessing vulnerability, potential abuse situations, and overt abuse.\n- **Vulnerability to Abuse Screening Scale (VASS)**: Self-report measure.\n- **Clinical judgment**: In this case, the physical findings alone are sufficient to raise high suspicion without formal screening tools.\n\n**Risk Factors Present**:\n- Social isolation (cut off from family and community)\n- Caregiver financial dependence (son is unemployed, living in her home)\n- Caregiver substance use (should be assessed — financial problems and job loss)\n- Cognitive impairment (mild — reduces ability to self-advocate)\n- Recent widowhood (lost the protective factor of a spouse)\n- Functional dependence (creates power imbalance)',
