      content:
          '**Immediate Medical Actions**:\n\n**1. Medical Evaluation and Treatment**:\n- **Document all injuries**: Detailed description with body diagram. Photograph all bruises with patient consent (with ruler for scale). Note the pattern, size, color (estimate age), and location of each injury.\n- **Laboratory workup**: CBC (anemia from malnutrition or occult bleeding), CMP (renal function, electrolytes — dehydration), albumin/prealbumin (nutritional status), coagulation studies (rule out bleeding disorder), thyroid function, vitamin D and B12 levels, urinalysis.\n- **Imaging**: Consider skeletal survey if concern for occult fractures (similar to child abuse workup). Rib X-rays given flank and rib bruising.\n- **Medication reconciliation**: Restart amlodipine (BP 168/94 — dangerously uncontrolled), atorvastatin, and sertraline. Her son\'s unilateral decision to stop medications is medical neglect.\n- **Nutrition**: Immediate dietary intervention — high-calorie, high-protein supplements (Ensure, Boost). Referral to dietitian. Consider oral nutritional supplementation.\n- **Cognitive assessment**: Repeat formal cognitive testing — worsening from MMSE 24 to 22 could reflect depression, medication changes, malnutrition, or genuine cognitive decline. Differentiate with geriatric psychiatry referral.\n- **Depression treatment**: Restart sertraline. PHQ-9 screening. Assess for suicidal ideation.\n\n**2. Mandatory Reporting**:\n- **Elder abuse is a mandatory reporting obligation in ALL 50 states** for healthcare providers.\n- **Report to Adult Protective Services (APS)**: Call the APS hotline. Provide: patient name, location, specific concerns (physical abuse — describe injuries, neglect — malnutrition/medication withholding, financial exploitation — missing income), alleged perpetrator (son).\n- **You are NOT required to prove abuse** — only to report reasonable suspicion. APS investigates.\n- **Reporting is not optional and cannot be deferred** because the patient is reluctant or because you want to \'gather more information.\' You have sufficient evidence to report now.\n- **Document the report**: Date, time, APS reference number, name of person you spoke with.\n- **Inform the patient**: She needs to know you are making a report. Frame it as protection, not betrayal.\n\n**3. Immediate Safety Assessment**:\n- **Is she safe to go home with this son today?** If there is immediate danger, she should not be discharged to his care.\n- **Options**: (a) Admit to hospital for medical stabilization (malnutrition, uncontrolled hypertension) — this buys time for APS investigation. (b) Emergency placement in a safe environment (adult foster care, temporary shelter). (c) Contact the daughter — she may be unaware of the situation.\n- **APS may conduct an emergency investigation** and recommend immediate separation if danger is imminent.\n\n**4. Capacity Assessment**:\n- She has mild cognitive impairment but appears to retain decision-making capacity for basic choices.\n- If she has capacity and refuses intervention, she has the right to return home — but you must still report, document, and provide safety resources.\n- The financial POA should be reviewed by APS — if obtained through coercion, it may be invalid.\n\n**5. Safety Planning with the Patient**:\n- Emergency contact numbers (APS, 911, the daughter\'s phone number if she consents).\n- Code word with a neighbor or friend to signal she needs help.\n- Hide a small amount of cash and a pre-paid phone where the son will not find them.\n- Local elder abuse hotline number.\n- Discuss willingness to contact her daughter.',
