    Section(
      title: '+ How do you safely separate a suspected elder abuse victim from the suspected abuser in a clinical setting, and how do you then communicate with the patient?',
      content:
          '**Model Response**:\n\n**Phase 1: Separating the Patient from the Son**\n\n**1. Be firm but non-confrontational with the son.**\n"I appreciate that you are involved in your mother\'s care, and I understand you want to be helpful. It is standard practice in our clinic to examine patients privately for part of the visit. This is something we do with every patient, regardless of the circumstances. I need to do a complete physical examination, and hospital policy requires that examinations be conducted with the patient and the clinical team only."\n\n**2. If he resists:**\n"I understand your concern about her confusion, but I am able to communicate with her effectively, and if I need clarification, I will bring you back in afterward. This is a non-negotiable part of our clinical protocol. Our medical assistant will show you to the waiting area, and I will come get you when the examination is complete."\n\n**3. If he becomes aggressive or refuses:**\n"I hear that you feel strongly about this. I want to assure you that your mother is in good hands. However, I cannot proceed with the examination with anyone else in the room. If you would prefer, I can reschedule for another day, but this is our standard of care."\n[If he still refuses, do NOT back down. Signal the medical assistant to contact security if needed. The patient\'s safety is the priority.]\n\n**4. Have staff stay with the son.**\n- A medical assistant or social worker should remain in the waiting area to observe the son\'s behavior and ensure he does not attempt to re-enter the exam room.\n- If he leaves the building with the patient\'s belongings (coat, purse), this is a red flag for escalating control.\n\n**Phase 2: Communicating with the Patient Privately**\n\n**1. Create safety and calm.**\n[Sit at her level. Speak gently. Do not rush.]\n"Mrs. [Name], it is just you and me now. I want you to know that nothing you say in this room will be shared with your son without your permission. This is a safe and private conversation."\n\n**2. Assess safety with direct, non-judgmental questions.**\n"I am going to ask you some questions that I ask all of my patients. Please answer as honestly as you can.\n- Do you feel safe at home?\n- Has anyone hit you, kicked you, or hurt you physically?\n- Has anyone taken your money or belongings without your permission?\n- Are you getting enough to eat?\n- Has anyone stopped you from seeing people you want to see?\n- Are you afraid of anyone in your life right now?"\n[Pause after each question. Give her time to answer.]\n\n**3. If she discloses (or partially discloses):**\n"Thank you for telling me that. I believe you. What is happening to you is not okay, and it is not your fault. You deserve to be safe, to have enough food, and to see the people you love. I want to help you, and there are people whose job it is to protect people in your situation."\n\n**4. If she minimizes or denies:**\n"I understand. I want you to know that the bruises I see on your body are concerning to me. They are in patterns that are not typical of falls. I am not trying to get anyone in trouble — I am trying to make sure you are safe. If you ever feel unsafe or want help, you can call this number at any time [provide card]. You can also tell any nurse or doctor at any visit, and we will help you."\n\n**5. Explain mandatory reporting with compassion.**\n"Mrs. [Name], I want to be honest with you. As a doctor, when I see injuries and circumstances that concern me for a patient\'s safety, I am required by law to contact an agency called Adult Protective Services. This is not to punish you or your son — it is to make sure you are protected. They will come to talk with you, and their goal is to keep you safe. I am going to make that call today. Is there anything you want to tell me before I do?"\n\n**6. Empower her.**\n"You told me earlier that your son \'knows better\' than you. I have been talking to you for the last 20 minutes, and I want you to know — you are clear, you are thoughtful, and you know exactly what is happening in your life. You are not confused. You deserve to make your own decisions, and I am going to help you do that."\n\n**Key Principles**: Non-negotiable private examination (clinical standard of care — the abuser cannot override this). Direct, non-judgmental safety screening. Believed and validated the patient. Explained mandatory reporting transparently. Empowered the patient by affirming her cognitive capacity and autonomy. Did not confront the son directly about abuse (this could escalate danger to the patient after the visit).',
